displays the results of combining various prior specifications with discount factors for the multivariate models discussed in Section \ref{sec3}. Comparing weakly informative priors (e.g., $\bm C_{x_0} = \text{diag}(100)$) with more informative ones (e.g., $\bm C_{x_0} = \text{diag}(1)$) reveals a reduction in posterior uncertainty in the latter case, particularly at younger ages. This reduction can affect the distance between the fitted curves, either bringing them closer or pushing them apart, depending on the strength of the prior information and the chosen discount factor.}{ In this context, it may be of particular interest to perform a sensitivity analysis to assess whether more informative priors, in combination with age-varying discount factors, improve the model fit by reducing uncertainty and helping to prevent cross-overs. Although not displayed, the joint model with a latent common term yielded nearly identical results.


