\begin{abstract}
%Measuring the contribution of a bank or an insurance company to the overall systemic risk of the market is an important issue, especially in the aftermath of the 2007-2009 Financial Crisis and the financial downturn of 2020. Specifically, the Office of the Superintendent of Financial Institutions (OSFI) in Canada recommends a multifaceted approach to address this problem, with two major components being the size of the company and its interconnectedness within the market.

%One appropriate quantitative measure of systemic risk contribution is the marginal expected shortfall (MES), introduced by \cite{acharya2017measuring} and known to actuarial scholars as the risk capital allocation rule based on the expected shortfall risk measure since at least \cite{panjer2001solvency}. The MES risk measure has attracted considerable attention and has been evaluated under various assumptions on the joint probability distribution of companies' risks in the market of interest. However, in reality, the univariate probability distributions of distinct companies in the market can be quite distinct, and the dependence structure between the companies is rarely known.

Measuring the contribution of a bank or an insurance company to the overall systemic risk of the market is an important issue, especially in the aftermath of the 2007-2009 financial crisis and the financial downturn of 2020. In this paper, we derive the worst-case and best-case bounds for marginal expected shortfall (MES)---a key measure of systemic risk contribution---under the assumption of known marginal distributions for individual companies' risks but an unknown dependence structure. We further derive improved bounds for the MES risk measure when partial information on companies' risk exposures---and hence their dependence---is available. To capture this partial information, we utilize three commonly used background risk models: the additive, minimum-based, and multiplicative factor models. Finally, we present an alternative set of improved MES bounds based on a linear regression relationship between individual companies' risks and overall market risk, consistent with the assumptions of the Capital Asset Pricing Model in finance and the Weighted Insurance Pricing Model in insurance.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords:} Marginal expected shortfall, Risk bounds, Dependence, Systemic risk, Aggregate risk.

\section{Introduction}
Understanding and measuring systemic risk, that is, the risk that the failure of a single component could destabilize the entire system, has become a central concern in finance, insurance, and risk management, especially in the aftermath of the 2007–2009 financial crisis and the global economic disruption caused by the COVID-19 pandemic. For example, Canada’s Office of the Superintendent of Financial Institutions (OSFI) explicitly highlights the importance of addressing systemic risk arising from `concentration' in its 2024–2025 Annual Risk Outlook \citep{osfi2024riskoutlook}.

A key aspect of systemic risk measurement is assessing the contribution of individual institutions to overall market risk. In the insurance sector, this poses a significant challenge for industry-funded guarantee schemes---also called Policyholder Protection Schemes (PPSs)---such as Canada’s Property and Casualty Insurance Compensation Corporation (PACICC), State Guaranty Associations (SGAs) in the United States, and National Guarantee Schemes (NGSs) across the European Union. More specifically, PPSs charge premiums to their member insurers and provide protection to policyholders by covering claims and unearned premiums in the event of a member insurer’s insolvency. At present, virtually all PPSs calculate the said premiums primarily based on each member insurer’s direct written premiums in covered lines of business---a practice followed by, e.g., PACICC and various SGAs in the United States. However, this approach is limited in that it does not account for an individual member insurer’s contribution to the aggregate systemic risk. Recognizing this gap, the International Association of Insurance Supervisors (IAIS) has encouraged the development of risk-based premium assessment frameworks for PPSs, as recently discussed in \citet{iais2023pps}.

The marginal expected shortfall (MES) has emerged as a widely used measure of systemic risk contribution. Initially proposed by \citet{acharya2017measuring}---and long beforehand recognized in actuarial science \citep{denault2001coherent,panjer2001solvency} as a risk capital allocation (RCA) rule based on the expected shortfall (ES) risk measure---MES has received considerable attention in both research and applications. Speaking more formally, let the RVs (RVs) $X_1,X_2\ldots,X_d,\ d\in\mathbb{N}$ denote the individual risks associated with insurers labeled $\mathcal{N}=\{1,2,\ldots,d\}$, and let the RV $S=X_1+X_2+\cdots+X_d$ represent the aggregate risk in the market. Further, assume that all the aforementioned RVs have continuous cumulative distribution functions and finite means. The ES risk measure of the aggregate (systemic) risk, for a prudence level $p\in(0,\ 1)$ and the Value-at-Risk (VaR) risk measure $\rm{VaR}_p(S)$, is defined as 
\begin{equation*}
\label{ES-intro-def}
\mathrm{ES}_p(S)=\mathbb{E}[S\vert\ S> \rm{VaR}_p(S)],
\end{equation*}
\citep[e.g.,][]{denuit2006actuarial}, and the corresponding MES risk measure is defined as
\begin{equation*}
\label{MES-intro-def}
\mathrm{MES}_p(X_j,S)=\mathbb{E}[X_j\vert\ S> \rm{VaR}_p(S)],\ j\in\mathcal{N},
\end{equation*}
\citep{acharya2017measuring}. 

	The theoretical properties of both the ES and MES functionals have been thoroughly studied. In particular, the former functional has been shown to be coherent \citep{artzner1999coherent,acerbi2002expected}, comonotonic additive \citep{kusuoka2001law}, convex \citep{follmer2002convex}, elicitable \citep{fissler2016higher}, and uniquely characterized by a set of `economic' axioms, justifying its role as a leading regulatory risk measure \citep{wang2021axiomatic}. Furthermore, it is `distorted' in the sense of \citet{wang2000distortion} and `weighted' in the sense of \citet{furman2008weightedb}. Finally, the Solvency III regulatory framework---based on Basel III, which is known as the Fundamental Review of the Trading Book \citep{basel2016es}---is expected to replace the VaR risk measure at the prudence level $p=0.99$ with the ES risk measure at the prudence level $p=0.975$ for calculating market risk capital. The latter functional, that is, the MES risk measure, inherits much of the appeal of the ES risk measure on which it is based \citep[e.g.,][and references therein]{tsanakas2003risk,furman2008weighted,dhaene2012optimal}.

On a more application-oriented front, a substantial body of literature has derived explicit formulas for the ES and MES functionals under specific assumptions about the joint cumulative distribution function of the risk RVs $X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_d,\ d \in \mathbb{N}$. In particular, \citet{panjer2001solvency} provided formulations for the multivariate normal distribution, while \citet{landsman2003tail} and \cite{dhaene2008some} extended these results to the broader class of elliptical distributions. \citet{furman2005risk} \citep[also][]{zhou2018approximation} derived results for a class of multivariate gamma distributions based on additive background risk models, and \citet{furman2010multivariate} further extended these findings to a more general class of multivariate Tweedie distributions. The multivariate phase-type and multivariate Pareto distributions were addressed in \citet{cai2005conditional} and \citet{vernic2006multivariate,vernic2011tail}, respectively. More recently, \citet{landsman2016tail} and \citet{ignatieva2019conditional} derived formulations for risk variables following multivariate log-elliptical and skewed generalized hyperbolic distributions, and \citet{cossette2018dependent} and \citet{marri2022risk} considered the case of dependent risks modeled via Archimedean and mixed Bernstein copulas. It should be noted that this overview is far from exhaustive. A considerable body of additional literature has explored ES and MES in diverse modeling frameworks beyond those mentioned here.

Specific assumptions on the joint cumulative distribution of risks are more of a mathematical nicety than a reflection of reality. In practice, the form of dependence that governs the joint behaviour of the risk RVs $X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_d,\ d\in\mathbb{N}$---and hence the distribution of the aggregate risk RV $S$---is rarely known. A plethora of authors have addressed this dependence uncertainty in the context of the aggregate risk RV, $S$. For instance, \citet{bernard2014risk} derived a lower bound for $\mathrm{ES}_p(S)$ for homogeneous risks by leveraging properties of complete mixability \citep[e.g.,][]{wang2011complete} and coherent risk measures \citep{artzner1999coherent}. \cite{jakobsons2016general} further discovered the minimum $\mathrm{ES}_p(S)$ for heterogeneous risks under the assumptions of jointly mixable and continuous marginal distributions \citep[e.g.,][]{wang2016joint}. Moreover, \cite{wang2021axiomatic} proposed the concept of $p$-concentration, which refers to the absence of diversification benefits in portfolio construction, and illustrated the upper bound of $\mathrm{ES}_p(S)$ for any risks through comonotonicity (\cite{dhaene2002concept} and \cite{deelstra2011overview} discussed generally the comonotonicity and its applications in finance and insurance).

However, the impact of dependence uncertainty on the systemic risk contribution, as measured by MES, remains largely unexplored. Understanding the lower and upper bounds of $\mathrm{MES}_p(X_j, S)$ is critically important for PPSs and financial regulators, particularly given the growing prominence of systemic risks, such as cyber threats, climate-related disasters, global pandemics, and geopolitical instability, in today’s interconnected risk landscape. Quantifying these bounds provides key insights into worst-case scenarios and supports the development of more resilient financial and insurance systems. 

In this paper, we address this gap by studying the effect of dependence uncertainty on MES. Our contributions to the literature are as follows: First, sharp lower and upper bounds for MES---along with the corresponding copulas that achieve these bounds---are derived under complete dependence uncertainty. Such bounds quantify the maximum and minimum systemic risk contribution of an individual company's risks under extreme scenarios. Second, we derive improved risk bounds for MES under constrained dependence uncertainty. The degree of dependence uncertainty can be reduced by incorporating factor models or imposing additional constraints that provide partial information about the joint behaviour of companies' risks. Examples include the additive \citep[][]{gollier1996risk,furman2005risk}, multiplicative \citep[][]{franke2006multiplicative,asimit2016background}, and minimum-based \citep[][]{marshall1967multivariate,pai2020livestock} background risk models,  all of which are commonly used in insurance, and, more generally, in quantitative risk management.

More specifically, in the first part of this paper (Section~\ref{no information}), we study sharp upper and lower bounds on $\mathrm{MES}_p(X_j,S)$ under the assumption that only the marginal information is available, that is, the marginal distributions of $X_i$, $i\in\mathcal{N},$ are known, but the dependence of the risk vector $(X_1,X_2,\dots,X_d),\ d\in\mathbb{N}$ is completely unspecified. In the second part of this paper (Section~\ref{factor models sec}), we derive improved bounds for the MES risk measure by incorporating partial dependence information through the structural assumption of a factor model.  To this end, we examine three commonly used background risk models---additive, multiplicative, and minimum-based---to demonstrate that adding dependence information tighten the range of feasible MES values. We conclude the paper by analyzing MES bounds assuming a linear relationship between $\mathbb{E}[X_i \vert S],\ i \in \mathcal{N}$, and the systemic risk RV, $S$ (Section~\ref{linear condition}). This assumption is central to both the Capital Asset Pricing Model \citep[][]{levy2011capm} and the Weighted Insurance Pricing Model \citep[][]{furman2017beyond}.

\section{Risk bounds under known marginal distributions and unknown dependence}\label{no information}
The primary objective of this section is to derive lower and upper bounds for the MES when the marginal distributions of the risk variables are known, but the dependence structure among them is unspecified.

\subsection{Preliminaries}
Let \((\Omega, \mathcal{A}, \mathbb{P})\) denote a standard atomless probability space, and let $L^0 := L^0(\Omega, \mathcal{A}, \mathbb{P})$ represent the set of all real-valued RVs $X,\ Y,\ X_i,\ i\in\mathcal{N},\ d \in \mathbb{N}$ on this probability space. Throughout this paper, the notation $X\sim F_X$ indicates that the RV $X$ follows the cumulative distribution function \(F_X(x) = \mathbb{P}(X \leq x), \, x \in \mathbb{R}\). The function \(F_i^{-1}(p)\) denotes the quantile function of \(F_i\) defined as  
\[
F_i^{-1}(p) = \inf \{x \in \mathbb{R} : F_i(x) \geq p \}, \quad p \in (0, 1).
\] Unless otherwise specified, we assume that all RVs involved have finite expectations, and $U$ is a standard uniformly distributed RV. The value-at-risk (VaR) of a RV $X$ at the prudence level $p$, denoted by $\text{VaR}_p(X)$, is
\begin{equation*}
	\text{VaR}_p(X)=\inf\{x\in\mathbb{R}: F_X(x)\geq p\}.
\end{equation*}
In this section, we consider the problems
\begin{align}
	m(p)&=\inf \left\{\mathrm{MES}_p(X_j,S): \bm{X}\in \mathcal{A}(\bm{X}), S=\sum_{i=1}^{d}X_i \right\}, \label{m} \\
	M(p)&=\sup \left\{\mathrm{MES}_p(X_j,S): \bm{X}\in \mathcal{A}(\bm{X}), S=\sum_{i=1}^{d}X_i\right\},\label{M}
\end{align} where
\begin{equation*}
	\mathcal{A}(\bm{X})=\left\{\bm{X}\in \mathcal{X}^d:X_i\sim F_i, 1\leq i\leq d\right\}
\end{equation*} is the model with marginal information only.

Problems involving risk bounds under dependence uncertainty have been extensively studied in the literature, particularly in the context of the aggregate risk variable $S$. For example, \cite{embrechts2014academic} provide a comprehensive discussion and numerical illustrations of such problems in risk management. \cite{wang2011complete} derive a lower bound for $\mathbb{E}[f(S)]$, where $f$ is a convex function, and the marginals of $X_i,\ i\in\mathcal{N}$, are completely mixable. More recently, \cite{bernard2023coskewness} study sharp lower and upper bounds on the expectation of the product of $d\geq 3$ RVs under some specific marginal distributions.

\subsection{Unconstrained risk bounds}
The quantity $M(p)$ in \eqref{M} is well-known due to a property of capital allocation rules known as 'no-undercut'. That is, no individual risk $X_i,\ i\in\mathcal{N}$ is allocated more capital than it would receive if considered in isolation \citep{furman2008weighted}. We first provide an exact characterization of $M(p)$ in the following theorem.
\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]\label{upper bound}
	Let $X_i\sim F_i$, $i\in\mathcal{N},$ denote $d\in\mathbb{N}$ risks, and $S=\sum_{i=1}^{d}X_i$ represent the aggregate risk. The upper bound of $\mathrm{MES}_p(X_i, S)$ is 
	\begin{equation}\label{upper bounds for d risk}
		M(p)=\mathrm{ES}_p\left(X_i\right).
	\end{equation} Furthermore, the upper bound is obtained when $X_i=F_i^{-1}(U)$, in which $U\sim U[0,1]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
	Let $x_{j,p}=\text{VaR}_p(X_j)$ and $s_p=\text{VaR}_p(S)$. Note that $\mathbb{P}(X_j>x_{j,p})=\mathbb{P}(S>s_p)=1-p$. We have \begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\mathrm{ES}_p\left(X_j\right)=&x_{j,p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert X_j>x_{j,p}] \\
			=&x_{j,p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert X_j>x_{j,p},S>s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(S>s_p\vert X_j>x_{j,p}\right)\\
			&+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert X_j>x_{j,p},S\leq s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(S\leq s_p\vert X_j>x_{j,p}\right)\\
			\geq&x_{j,p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert X_j>x_{j,p},S>s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(S>s_p\vert X_j>x_{j,p}\right)\\
			=&x_{j,p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert X_j>x_{j,p},S>s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(X_j>x_{j,p}\vert S>s_p\right)\\
			\geq & x_{j,p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert X_j>x_{j,p},S>s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(X_j>x_{j,p}\vert S>s_p\right)\\
			&+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert X_j\leq x_{j,p},S>s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(X_j\leq x_{j,p}\vert S>s_p\right)\\
			=&x_{j,p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,p}\vert S>s_p]=\mathbb{E}[X_j\vert S>s_p]\\
			=&\mathrm{MES}_p\left(X_j,S\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*} When $X_i$, $i\in\mathcal{N},$ are comonotonic, the inequality changes to equality. In this case,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{aligned}
			\mathbb{P}\left(S\leq s_p\vert X_j>x_{j,p}\right)=&\frac{1}{1-p}\mathbb{P}\left(S\leq s_p, X_j>x_{j,p}\right)=\frac{1}{1-p}\mathbb{P}\left(U\leq p, U>p\right)\\
			=&0\\
			=&\frac{1}{1-p}\mathbb{P}\left(U\leq p, U>p\right)=\frac{1}{1-p}\mathbb{P}\left(X_j\leq x_{j,p}, S>s_p\right)\\
			=&\mathbb{P}\left(X_j\leq x_{j,p}\vert S>s_p\right).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation*}
	This completes the proof. 	
\end{proof}
The proof of Theorem~\ref{upper bound} follows closely the argument used in Property~2.4.7 of \cite{denuit2006actuarial}. The upper bound of $\mathrm{MES}_p(X_j, S)$ is always sharp by the comonotonicity, and it is independent of the dimension of the random vector. This upper bound is explicit and straightforward to compute once the marginal distribution function of \(X_j\) is known. Notably, the worst-case MES of \(X_j\) at prudence level \(p\) coincides with the expected shortfall (ES) of the marginal risk at the same level. However, the comonotonic random vector does lead to sharp upper bounds for many kinds of risk measures---expected shortfall included (see, e.g., \cite{meilijson1979convex} and \cite{bernard2017risk}).

Before presenting the next theorem, which derives a lower bound of $\mathrm{MES}_p\left(X_j,S\right)$, we introduce the left expected shortfall as a measure of left-tail risk. 
\begin{definition}[Left expected shortfall]
	The left expected shortfall (LES) of a RV $X$ at the prudence level $p$, denoted by $\mathrm{LES}_p(X)$, is
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathrm{LES}_p(X)=\mathbb{E}[X\vert X\leq\text{VaR}_p(X)]=\frac{\mathbb{E}[X\mathds{1}_{X\leq\text{VaR}_p(X)}]}{p},
	\end{equation*} where $\mathds{1}_{\mathcal{A}}$ is the indicator function of event $\mathcal{A}$.
\end{definition}
\begin{theorem}[Lower bound]\label{lower bound}
	Let $X_i\sim F_i$, $i\in\mathcal{N},$ represent a collection of $d\in\mathbb{N}$ risks, and $S=\sum_{i=1}^{d}X_i$ denote the aggregate risk. The lower bound of $\mathrm{MES}_p(X_j, S)$ is given by
	\begin{equation}\label{lower bounds for d risks}
		m(p)\geq \mathrm{LES}_{1-p}(X_j).
	\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
	Let $x_{j,1-p}=\text{VaR}_{1-p}(X_j)$ and $s_p=\text{VaR}_{p}(S)$. We have \begin{equation}\label{lower bound inequalities}
		\begin{aligned}
			\mathrm{LES}_{1-p}(X_j)=&\mathbb{E}[X_j\vert X_j\leq x_{j,1-p}]\\
			=&\frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\mathbb{E}[X_j\vert X_j> x_{j,1-p}]\\
			=&\frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\left\{x_{j,1-p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}]\right\} \\
			=&\frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\left\{x_{j,1-p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}, S>s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(S> s_p\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}\right)\right.\\
			&+\left.\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}, S\leq s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(S\leq s_p\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}\right)\right\}\\
			\leq&\frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\left\{x_{j,1-p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}, S\leq s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(S\leq s_p\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}\right)\right\}\\
			=&\frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\left\{x_{j,1-p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}, S\leq s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(X_j>x_{j,1-p}\vert S\leq s_p \right)\right\}\\
			\leq & \frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\left\{x_{j,1-p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert X_j>x_{j,1-p}, S\leq s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(X_j>x_{j,1-p}\vert S\leq s_p \right)\right.\\
			&+\left.\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert X_j\leq x_{j,1-p}, S\leq s_p]\mathbb{P}\left(X_j\leq x_{j,1-p}\vert S\leq s_p \right)\right\}\\
			=&\frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\left\{x_{j,1-p}+\mathbb{E}[X_j-x_{j,1-p}\vert S\leq s_p]\right\}\\
			=&\frac{\mathbb{E}[X_j]}{1-p}-\frac{p}{1-p}\mathbb{E}[X_j\vert S\leq s_p]=\mathbb{E}[X_j\vert S> s_p]\\
			=&\mathrm{MES}_q(X_j, S).
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation} Note that $\mathbb{P}(X_j>x_{j,1-p})=\mathbb{P}(S\leq s_p)=p$.
	This completes the proof. 	
\end{proof}
Inequality~\eqref{lower bounds for d risks} implies that the minimum systemic risk contribution of an individual risk $X_j$ should not be lower than the conditional expected value of that stand-alone risk, given that the loss does not exceed its VaR at the prudence level \(1 - p\).

