\section{Conclusion} \label{conclusion}
In this paper, we investigate the systemic risk contribution measure known as the marginal expected shortfall (MES), a critical tool in financial and insurance risk management, particularly in light of the 2007–2009 financial crisis and the financial downturn of 2020. We first derive risk bounds for MES under the assumption that marginal distributions are fixed, but dependence structure is entirely unknown. While the upper bound is attained under comonotonicity, the lower bound is not necessarily sharp. We establish the necessary and sufficient conditions for achieving this lower bound.  

Subsequently, we introduce partial dependence information through structural risk factor models to obtain more practical and reduced risk bounds. Our analytical results indicate that incorporating such information may improve the bounds. Moreover, the dependence uncertainty spread---the gap between the upper and lower bounds---decreases compared to the case with no dependence information. Additionally, we derive improved MES risk bounds under the assumption that aggregate risk and the conditional expectation of all relevant RVs (conditioned on that risk) exhibit a linear relationship. In this setting, the partial dependence information added is implicit, yet we provide explicit bounds for non-negative RVs.  

Our findings demonstrate that incorporating partial dependence information meaningfully influences the risk bounds of MES, aligning with established results for VaR and ES in the existing literature. These insights contribute to a more comprehensive understanding of systemic risk assessment and offer practical implications for financial and insurance risk management.

