\subsection{Sinogram restoration network}
The proposed deep network was inspired by a segmentation task~\citep{Myronenko2019} and consists of a residual U-Net~\citep{zhang2018road} combining the strengths of residual learning~\citep{he2016deep} and the well-known U-Net architecture~\citep{U-net}. 
The network consists of an asymmetric encoder-decoder structure (Figure~\ref{fig:ResUnet}). 
The encoder layers feature an increasing number of residual blocks and one strided convolution, which halves the spatial dimensions. 
The layers of the decoder are composed of strided transposed convolutions which double the spatial dimensions, followed by one residual block. 
Skip connections connect the down-sampling and up-sampling segments, allowing the network to combine positional and contextual information to predict the missing sinogram information and aid gradient back-propagation during training. 
Each sinogram was normalised to the range $[0:1]$ before input and then denormalised by the same factor after output to control the unboundedness of the sinogram values. 
This was shown to be beneficial during training, achieving a more steady and fast convergence~\citep{lecun2002efficient}. 
Finally, the pixels not affected by the removed detectors were copied from the input sinograms and reinstated in the output.

