\section{Introduction}
% As AI enabled systems begin to be used across many industries, there is a growing requirement for these systems to be usable by a broad range of non-technical users. In scenarios where these systems interact with people, SLEEC rules, relating to Social, Legal, Ethical, Empathetic and Cultural factors play an important role in determining how the systems should behave. 

% However, due to the wide range of disciplines involved in eliciting these SLEEC rules, in many cases the person, or team, defining the system’s rules and capabilities do not have a technical understanding of the model checking tools used to verify their compliance. These tools assist in the elicitation, specification, validation and verification of SLEEC requirements and utilise formal and mathematical methods to reason about their correctness. Currently these models present their outputs in mathematical notation which is not intuitive for users without a technical background. Therefore, there is a need for the outputs of these tools to be easily translated into language that non-technical users can understand and work with. For example, in self-driving technology, the user responsible for determining the traffic rules of an autonomous vehicle likely has a deep understanding of road traffic laws, but little technical knowledge of requirements compliance. In a case like this, having a tool to translate potential logical errors in the traffic rules provided into simple human-readable language could prove very valuable.

% The recent growth in the field of Large Language Models (LLMs) provides an exciting opportunity to bridge this gap. Capable of generating human readable language from user prompts, LLMs are already proving successful in a range of fields such as automated customer service and real-time language translation. Key to the success of using an LLM for an output translation task is the correct prompt. This prompt engineering process involves carefully structuring the LLM prompt making sure to include things like contextual information, defined output structures and examples in order to improve the correctness and explainability of the LLMs outputs. For the end user this will provide more clarity in understanding the requirements inconsistencies, thus allowing these issues to be resolved faster. 
Normative requirements specify social, legal, ethical, empathetic, and cultural (SLEEC) norms that must be observed by a system. To support the identification of SLEEC requirements, numerous standards and regulations have been developed (e.g.,\cite{eu_ai_act_2024a}). These requirements are typically defined by stakeholders in the non-technical system with diverse expertise (e.g., ethicists, lawyers, social scientists). Hence, ensuring their consistency and managing the requirement elicitation process are complex and error-prone tasks\cite{TOWNSEND2025PGYNC,troquard2024social}. Recent research has addressed this challenge using domain-specific languages to specify normative requirements as rules, whose consistency can then be analyzed with formal methods~\cite{Getir-Yaman-et-al-23,GETIRYAMAN2025RCCPT,feng-et-al-24-e,feng-et-al-24bb}.

Nevertheless, these approaches often present the results from formal verification tools in a way that is inaccessible to non-technical users. This hinders understanding and makes the iterative process of eliciting and validating these requirements inefficient in terms of both time and effort. 

To address this problem, we introduce SLEEC-LLM, a tool that uses large language models (LLMs) to provide natural-language interpretations for model-checking counterexamples corresponding to SLEEC rule inconsistencies~\cite{GETIRYAMAN2025RCCPT,Getir-Yaman-et-al-23}.
%
SLEEC-LLM improves the efficiency and explainability of normative requirements elicitation and consistency analysis. %Our tool enhances automated reasoning techniques for eliciting and analyzing the consistency of normative requirements by leveraging LLMs to improve the explainability of the requirements elicitation process. 
To demonstrate its effectiveness, we summarise its use in two real-world case studies involving non-technical stakeholders.


\section{Problem Description}
