\begin{abstract}
% Federated recommender systems (FedRec) have attracted significant attention due to their ability to provide personalized recommendations while preserving user privacy. Nevertheless, recent research indicates that FedRec systems are susceptible to poisoning attacks, where malicious participants deliberately inject crafted gradients to manipulate recommendations toward specific target items. Existing poisoning attacks typically target the entire user groups, significantly diminishing attack stealthiness and increasing detectability. Furthermore, in practical scenarios, attackers may prefer to only prompt target items to a particular subgroup, such as recommending health supplements to elderly users, to increase the success rate and enhance stealthiness. Motivated by this gap, we engage to perform attacking target user subgroups and propose a novel attack method named Spattack. Spattack builds upon a simulation-and-promotion strategy, which first simulates user embeddings of target/non-target subgroups and then prompts target items to the target user subgroups. To enhance the simulation process, we push the inter-group embeddings away based on contrastive learning and augment the target group's relevant item set based on clustering. To enhance the promotion process, we further propose adaptively tuning the optimization weights between target and non-target subgroups. Besides, an embedding alignment strategy is proposed to align the embeddings between the target items and the relevant items. We conduct extensive experiments across three real-world datasets, seven top-tier poisoning attacks, and seven representative defense methods. Results show that Spattack can consistently achieve high attack performance on the target user group while maintaining low impact on non-target users, even with extremely small proportion of malicious clients (0.1\%). Moreover, it achieves better recommendation performance and is also capable of bypassing current defenses.

Federated recommender systems (FedRec) have emerged as a promising solution for delivering personalized recommendations while safeguarding user privacy. However, recent studies have demonstrated their vulnerability to poisoning attacks, wherein adversarial clients can inject carefully crafted updates to manipulate global recommendations. Existing attacks typically target the entire user group, which compromises stealth and increases the risk of detection. In contrast, real-world adversaries may prefer to prompt target items to specific user subgroups, such as recommending health supplements to elderly users, in order to improve attack success rates while preserving stealth. Motivated by this gap, we introduce Spattack, the first targeted poisoning attack designed to manipulate recommendations for specific user subgroups in the federated setting. Specifically, Spattack adopts a two-stage approximation-and-promotion strategy, which first simulates user embeddings of target/non-target subgroups and then prompts target items to the target subgroups. To enhance the approximation stage, we push the inter-group embeddings away based on contrastive learning and augment the target group's relevant item set based on clustering. To enhance the promotion stage, we further propose to adaptively tune the optimization weights between target and non-target subgroups. Besides, an embedding alignment strategy is proposed to align the embeddings between the target items and the relevant items. We conduct comprehensive experiments on three real-world datasets, comparing Spattack against seven state-of-the-art poisoning attacks and seven representative defense mechanisms. Experimental results demonstrate that Spattack consistently achieves strong manipulation performance on the specific user subgroup, while incurring minimal impact on non-target users, even when only 0.1\% of users are malicious. Moreover, Spattack maintains competitive overall recommendation performance and exhibits strong resilience against existing mainstream defenses.

% In the simulation stage, it approximates the embedding distributions of both target and non-target users, enhancing inter-group separation via contrastive learning and expanding the target group’s candidate items through item clustering. In the promotion stage, it adaptively optimizes updates to affect only the target users. 


\end{abstract}

