\subsection*{Disentangling the role of inbound commuters in the scaling of crime}

To set a baseline for understanding how commuter flows relate to crime, we first establish how crime scales with population size, replicating the population--crime relationship reported in previous studies. We estimate the scaling exponent $\beta$ from $C \sim N^{\beta}$, where $C$ is the number of crimes and $N$ is the population size, focusing on theft and burglary (see Methods). Our estimates align with prior findings for the UK\cite{oliveira2021more}. We find that both crimes scale superlinearly with population, with $\beta = 1.22$ (95\% CI: 1.14--1.29) for theft and $\beta = 1.25$ (95\% CI: 1.18--1.32) for burglary (Fig.~\ref{fig2}ab). This superlinearity implies that a 1\% increase in population is associated with more than a 1\% increase in crime---a 1\% population growth corresponds to a 1.22\% increase in theft and 1.25\% in burglary.
