Based on this routine, node positioning in Gabriel cells is completed rapidly. Nodes need to be located in cells, i.e., determining the \texttt{cellIndex} attribute, to retrieve passages enclosing them. Without iteratively examining all cells or relying on complicated point location query algorithms such as trapezoidal maps \citep{mark2008computational}, the proximity of adjacent path nodes enables fast sequential node positioning. Suppose $\mathbf{x}_1$ is previously located and a new node $\mathbf{x}_2$ is linked to $\mathbf{x}_1$ through edge $(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$. To determine $\mathbf{x}_2.$\texttt{cellIndex}, it suffices to investigate what cells $(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ traverses and the cells to be tested are thus limited. As in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: Node Positioning in Cells}, starting from the cell $\mathbf{x}_1$ is inside, the script examines each Gabriel cell in the order $(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ traverses until reaching the one $\mathbf{x}_2$ is located in. Since the edge is generally short compared with cell sizes, only a small number of cells will be passed before termination, resulting in an efficient node positioning and passage traversal check procedure. Meanwhile, other related attributes of $\mathbf{x}_2$ are also determined in this positioning procedure as in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: Node Positioning in Cells}.


\subsubsection{Cost computation}
Denote the optimal path from the start $\mathbf{x}_0$ to $\mathbf{x}$ online as $\sigma'_\mathbf{x}$. \texttt{Cost}($\mathbf{x}$) returns the path cost from $\mathbf{x}$ attributes, i.e., $\texttt{Cost}(\mathbf{x}) = f_c(\sigma'_\mathbf{x})$. This cost is stored in $\mathbf{x}.\texttt{cost}$. $l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ represents the straight-line path from $\mathbf{x}_1$ to $\mathbf{x}_2$. Suppose $l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ is collision-free, \texttt{NewCost}$(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ computes the cost of the concatenated path $\sigma'_{\mathbf{x}_1} | l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ as the cost to come of $\mathbf{x}_2$ through $\mathbf{x}_1$. As $l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ is newly concatenated, the variables used in cost computation are first updated. For example, a temporary sorted passage width vector is assigned $\mathbf{p}' = \mathbf{p}_{\sigma'_{\mathbf{x}_1}}$ for the joined path.
$\mathbf{p}'$ needs to be updated if $l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ has traversed some passages. Then the cost is calculated according to the specific PTOPP type. See the detailed procedure in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: Compute New Cost} where the cost type is easily configurable.


\subsubsection{Subtree node attributes update}
A key operation to attain path optimality in many sampling-based planners is modifying edge connections locally in the neighborhood of newly sampled nodes, e.g., the rewire routine in RRT$^*$ and its variants. The effect of changing a node's edge connection propagates to its descendant nodes. Assume the edge $(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ is newly established by changing $\mathbf{x}_2$'s parent to $\mathbf{x}_1$. This modification influences $\mathbf{x}_2$'s descendants since optimal paths to them change accordingly. $\mathbf{x}_2$ is the root of the subtree composed of its descendant nodes. Attributes related to the optimal path to subtree nodes are updated using the new path $\sigma'_{\mathbf{x}_1} | l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2) | \sigma'(\mathbf{x}_2 \sim \mathbf{x})$, where $\sigma'(\mathbf{x}_2 \sim \mathbf{x})$ represents the path segment from $\mathbf{x}_2$ to $\mathbf{x}$ in the subtree. This can be done by traversing the subtree with breadth-first search (BFS) or DFS. \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}() in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: Subtree Update} details this procedure in a BFS backbone to update subtree node attributes of \texttt{len}, \texttt{cost}, and \texttt{sortedWidths}.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\SetNlSty{}{}{:}
\small{
    \nl Input $\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2$ with $l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ being collison-free\;
    \nl $\mathbf{p}' \leftarrow \mathbf{x}_1.\texttt{sortedWidths}$\;
    \nl $\text{len} = \mathbf{x}_1.\texttt{len} + \text{Len}(l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2))$\;
    \nl \If{\textnormal{\texttt{SameCell}$(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$} == False}{
        \nl $P_t \leftarrow$ passages $l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$ traverses\;
        \nl Insert passage widths in $P_t$ to $\mathbf{p}'$\;
    }
    \nl \Return path cost in PTOPP\;
    }
    \caption{\small \texttt{NewCost}$(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$}
    \label{Alg: Compute New Cost}
\end{algorithm}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\SetKw{logicalNot}{not}
\SetNlSty{}{}{:}
\small{
    \nl Input $\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2$ where $\mathbf{x}_1$ is the new parent of $\mathbf{x}_2$\;
    \nl \If{\textnormal{$\mathbf{x}_2.\texttt{parent} \neq$} NULL} {
        \nl $\mathbf{x}_2.$\texttt{parent}.\texttt{children}.remove($\mathbf{x}_2$)\;
        }
    \nl lenChange $\leftarrow \mathbf{x}_1.\texttt{len} + \text{Len}(l(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)) - \mathbf{x}_2.\texttt{len}$\;
    \nl \texttt{PositionNode}$(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$\;
    \nl nodeQueue $\leftarrow \{ \mathbf{x}_2 \}$\;
    \nl \While{\textnormal{nodeQueue} $\neq \emptyset$}{
        \nl $\mathbf{x} \leftarrow$ nodeQueue.front()\;
        \nl nodeQueue.removeFront()\;
        \nl \ForEach{\textnormal{$\mathbf{x}_{child} \in \mathbf{x}$.\texttt{children}}} {
            \nl $\mathbf{x}_{child}.\texttt{len} \leftarrow \mathbf{x}_{child}.\texttt{len} + \text{lenChange}$\;
            \nl $\mathbf{x}_{child}.\texttt{sortedWidths} \leftarrow \mathbf{x}.\texttt{sortedWidths}$\;
            \nl \If{$\mathbf{x}_{child}.\textnormal{\texttt{passedWidths}} \neq \emptyset$} {
                \nl Insert $\mathbf{x}_{child}.\texttt{passedWidths}$ into $\mathbf{x}_{child}.$\texttt{sortedWidths}\;
            }
            \nl $\mathbf{x}_{child}.\texttt{cost} \leftarrow \texttt{Cost}(\mathbf{x}_{child})$\;
            \nl nodeQueue.push($\mathbf{x}_{child}$)\;
        }
    }
    }
    \caption{\small \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}$(\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2)$}
    \label{Alg: Subtree Update}
\end{algorithm}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


\section{Sampling-based algorithms for PTOPP}
This section elaborates on sampling-based optimal planners for PTOPP integrating previous components. In particular, algorithmic integration with the two arguably most influential asymptotically optimal planners, optimal PRM (PRM$^*$) and optimal RRT (RRT$^*$) \citep{orthey2023sampling}, is detailed. The integration principles with their major planner variants are also briefly discussed. 


\subsection{PRM\texorpdfstring{$^*$}{*} and variants for PTOPP}
Primarily aimed at multi-query applications, PRM consists of the preprocessing phase constructing a roadmap from random samples in $\mathcal{X}_{free}$ and the query phase searching for the optimal path in this roadmap. It is probabilistically complete in finding feasible but non-optimal paths. To be asymptotically optimal, PRM$^*$ enhances roadmap construction by adopting a connection radius as a function of the total sample number. In PTOPP, roadmap construction remains unchanged to capture the connectivity of $\mathcal{X}_{free}$. The key to achieving the optimal path lies in modifying the path search procedure. Let the undirected graph $G = (V, E)$ with vertex set $V$ and edge set $E$ represent the roadmap. Before path searching, $\mathbf{x}_0$ and $\mathbf{x}_g$ are first linked the $G$. The nodes $\mathbf{x}_0$ and $\mathbf{x}_g$ connect to are found by minimizing the specified cost $f_c(\cdot)$ under path length regulation. Suppose $\mathbf{x}_0$ and $\mathbf{x}_g$ are linked to $\mathbf{x}_{G, 0}, \mathbf{x}_{G, g} \in V$ respectively, then
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn: PRM Connection Node Start}
\begin{split}
    &\mathbf{x}_{G, 0} = \operatorname*{arg \, min}_{\mathbf{x} \in V}  \, \text{Len}(l(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x})) \\
    \st \;\; &f_c(l(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x}_{G,0})) = \min  f_c(l(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x})) \\
            & \qquad l(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x}_{G,0}) \subset \mathcal{X}_{free}. \\
\end{split}    
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
$\mathbf{x}_{G,g}$ is obtained analogously by replacing $l(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x}_{G,0})$ with $l(\mathbf{x}_{G,g}, \mathbf{x}_g)$. Alternatively, $\mathbf{x}_0$ and $\mathbf{x}_g$ may also be directly added to the roadmap as nodes.
The query phase searches for the optimal path from $\mathbf{x}_{G, 0}$ to $\mathbf{x}_{G, g}$ in $G$. Because of the path cost monotonicity in PTOPP, $G$ is a non-negatively weighted graph. Therefore, optimal path finding equals the shortest path problem where edge weights depend on passages passed by edges. Shortest path finding techniques in weighted graphs can be adopted readily with modifications to compute node costs in PTOPP. 


The PTOPP pipeline built on PRM$^*$ is outlined in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: PRM* for PTOPP}. Passage and cell preprocessing is carried out as in Section \ref{Sec: Passage Detection}. $r_{PRM^*}$ represents the connection radius as a function of the batch sample number $n$ \citep{karaman2011sampling}, i.e., $r_{PRM^*} = \gamma_{PRM}(\log (n) / n)^{1/d}$ where $\gamma_{PRM} > \gamma_{PRM}^* = 2(1 + 1/d)^{1/d} (\mu(\mathcal{X}_{free}) / \xi_d))^{1/d}$. $d = \text{dim}(\mathcal{X})$ is the dimension of the space $\mathcal{X}$. $\mu(\mathcal{X}_{free})$ and $\xi_d$ denotes the volume of $\mathcal{X}_{free}$ and the unit ball in the $\mathbb{R}^d$ Euclidean space, respectively. Separate node localization in Gabriel cells is only performed for $\mathbf{x}_{G,0}$ initially. The query phase adopts Dijkstra's algorithm with two core substitute subroutines. First, the temporary path cost through node $\mathbf{u}$ to its neighbor $\mathbf{v}$ is calculated by \texttt{NewCost}$(\mathbf{u, v})$. Second, if the temporary path cost is smaller than $\mathbf{v}.$\texttt{cost}, \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}$(\mathbf{u, v})$ is invoked to update path connections and node attributes. In PRM$^*$, nodes maintain adjacent nodes, not $\texttt{children}$, \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}$(\mathbf{u, v})$ thus terminates without entering the BFS procedure. Note that under path length regulation, node precedence is compared lexicographically by the tuple (\texttt{cost}, \texttt{len}). $\mathbf{u}$ is considered to have a smaller PTOPP path cost than $\mathbf{v}$ if
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{equation}
\label{Eqn: Node Cost Comparison}
      (\mathbf{u}.\texttt{cost}, \mathbf{u}.\texttt{len}) \prec_{\text{lex}} (\mathbf{v}.\texttt{cost}, \mathbf{v}.\texttt{len}).
\end{equation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
This is utilized when determining the minimum cost node (line \ref*{AlgLine: Minimum Cost} in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: PRM* for PTOPP} with a priority queue) and comparing the temporary cost with node cost (line \ref*{AlgLine: Cost Update Condition} in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: PRM* for PTOPP}). At last, path nodes are retrieved by recursively fetching nodes' predecessor \texttt{parent}, reversing its order in \texttt{GetPath}(), and concatenating $\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x}_g$. 


The underlying architecture of PRM$^*$ for PTOPP depicts a generic pipeline of graph-based solution comprising roadmap construction and optimal path search. It applies to other optimal PRM variants, such as simplified PRM (sPRM), that only differ in the roadmap construction phase. In addition, one important branch is pure cell-based methods enabled by cell decomposition \citep{choset2005principles, lavalle2006planning}. They are particularly promising in PTOPP because no extra cell decomposition step is required. Rather than random samples, the roadmap uses sparse non-random samples in cells, e.g., centroids and cell edge midpoints, as vertices to capture the connectivity of divided regions. Because of the dramatic decrease in node number, pure cell-based methods enjoy high computational efficiency. The inherent downside is that the found path is coarse and subject to resolution optimality. Heavy path postprocessing is usually needed. It is also complicated to extend to high-dimensional spaces. % This article concentrates on random sampling-based optimal algorithms for PTOPP. 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\SetKw{logicalAnd}{and}
\SetKw{logicalOr}{or}
\SetNlSty{}{}{:}
\small{
    \nl Input $\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x}_g$ and conduct passage and cell preprocessing\;
    \tcp{preprocessing phase}
    \nl $V \leftarrow \{\texttt{SampleFree}()\}_{i = 1, ..., n}; E \leftarrow \emptyset$\;
    \nl \ForEach{$\mathbf{v} \in V$} {
        \nl $V_{near} \leftarrow \texttt{Near}(G = (V, E), \mathbf{v}, r_{PRM^*}) \backslash \{\mathbf{v}\}$\;
        \nl \ForEach{$\mathbf{u} \in V_{near}$} {
            \nl \If{\textnormal{\texttt{CollisionFree}}$(\mathbf{v, u})$} {
            \nl $E \leftarrow E \cup \{(\mathbf{v, u}), (\mathbf{u, v})\}$\;
            }
        }
    }
    \tcp{query phase}
    \nl $\mathbf{x}_{G, 0}, \mathbf{x}_{G, g} \leftarrow (\ref{Eqn: PRM Connection Node Start}); Q \leftarrow V; \mathbf{x}_{G, 0}.\texttt{cost} \leftarrow \texttt{Cost}(\mathbf{x}_{G, 0})$\;
    \nl Locate $\mathbf{x}_{G, 0}$ in Gabriel cells\;
    \nl \While{$Q \neq \emptyset$} {
        \nl \label{AlgLine: Minimum Cost}$\mathbf{u} \leftarrow$ node in $Q$ with the minimum \texttt{cost}\; 
        \nl \If{$\mathbf{u}$ == $\mathbf{x}_{G,g}$} {
            \nl break\;
        }
        \nl $Q \leftarrow Q \backslash \{ \mathbf{u} \}; A_u \leftarrow$ adjacency list of $\mathbf{u}$\;
        \nl \ForEach{$\mathbf{v} \in A_u$ \logicalAnd $\mathbf{v} \in Q$ } {
            \nl $\text{tempCost} \leftarrow \texttt{NewCost}(\mathbf{u, v})$\;
            \nl \label{AlgLine: Cost Update Condition}\If{\textnormal{tempCost} $< \mathbf{v}.\textnormal{\texttt{cost}}$ \\ \logicalOr \textnormal{(}\textnormal{tempCost} == \textnormal{$\mathbf{v}.\texttt{cost}$ \logicalAnd $\mathbf{u}.\texttt{Len} + \text{len}(l(\mathbf{u, v})) < \mathbf{v}.\texttt{len}$)}} {
                \nl \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}$(\mathbf{u, v})$;
            }
        }
    }
    \nl $ \sigma \leftarrow \texttt{GetPath}(\mathbf{x}_{G,0}, \mathbf{x}_{G,g})$\;
    \nl \Return $l(\mathbf{x}_0, \mathbf{x}_{G, 0}) \, | \, \sigma \, | \, l(\mathbf{x}_{G, g}, \mathbf{x}_g)$\;
    }
    \caption{\small PRM$^*$ for PTOPP}
    \label{Alg: PRM* for PTOPP}
\end{algorithm}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


\subsection{RRT\texorpdfstring{$^*$}{*} and variants for PTOPP}
RRT emerges as an online and single-query counterpart of PRM since it allows for incremental sampling during planning instead of batch sampling beforehand. It maintains a tree structure and is probabilistically complete but non-optimal. RRT$^*$ is the asymptotically optimal version of RRT by adding the rewiring procedure which locally modifies existing edge connections every time a new sample is inserted to ensure nodes are reached through the path of minimum cost from the root. In this way, the tree embeds optimal paths from the root for all nodes in the random tree growth process. To do this in PTOPP, the backbone of RRT$^*$ is retained. Meanwhile, core modifications are introduced similarly to those in PRM$^*$ for PTOPP to guarantee that the minimum cost path to each node is correctly maintained.


RRT$^*$ for PTOPP is detailed in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: RRT* for PTOPP} which reuses the architecture and basic routines of vanilla RRT$^*$. Again, the addition of \texttt{NewCost}() and \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}() is crucial for cost computation and dynamic tree maintenance. At first, $\mathbf{x}_0$ is inserted into $V$, localized in cells, and the optimal path cost $f_c$ is assigned to be infinity. The proposed algorithms are cost-agnostic and compatible with costs in Section \ref{Sec: Problem Categories}. The specific cost is conveniently customizable in \texttt{NewCost}(). \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}() is invoked when creating the edge along the minimum cost path to the newly sampled $\mathbf{x}_{new}$ and when adjusting existing edges in the rewire routine. It alters edges and propagates resulting attribute changes to descendant nodes. Lexicographic comparison of (\texttt{cost}, \texttt{len}) is used in rewire condition (line \ref*{AlgLine: Rewire Condition} in Algorithm \ref*{Alg: RRT* for PTOPP}). In implementation, updating $V$ and $E$ is accomplished implicitly by updating related node attributes, i.e., \texttt{parent} and \texttt{children}. To retrieve the optimal path, only the parent of $\mathbf{x}_g$ needs to be tracked. % Techniques like biasing samples to the goal may be utilized to accelerate finding paths.
\iffalse
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\SetKw{logicalAnd}{AND}
\SetKw{logicalOr}{OR}
\SetNlSty{}{}{:}
\small{
    \nl Input $\mathbf{x}_{new}$ and its conlision-free neighbor set $X_{near}$\;
    \nl \ForEach{$\mathbf{x}_{near} \in X_{near}$} {
        \nl \If{\textnormal{\texttt{NewCost}$(\mathbf{x}_{near}, \mathbf{x}_{new}) < \mathbf{x}_{near}.\texttt{cost}$ \logicalOr (\texttt{NewCost}$(\mathbf{x}_{near}, \mathbf{x}_{new}) == \mathbf{x}_{near}.\texttt{cost}$ \logicalAnd $\mathbf{x}_{new}.\texttt{len} + \text{Len}(l(\mathbf{x}_{new}, \mathbf{x}_{near})) < \mathbf{x}_{near}.\texttt{len}$)}} {
                \nl $E \leftarrow E \backslash \{ (\mathbf{x}_{near}.\texttt{parent}, \mathbf{x}_{near}) \} \cup \{ (\mathbf{x}_{new}, \mathbf{x}_{near}) \}$\; 
                \nl \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}$(\mathbf{x}_{new}, \mathbf{x}_{near})$\;
            }
        }
