\subsubsection{Asymptotic optimality}
Suppose OPP problems admit a robustly optimal solution, i.e., the optimal path $\sigma^*$ is homotopic to some feasible path with positive clearance to obstacles, and for any sequence of feasible paths $\{\sigma_n\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \sigma_n = \sigma^*$ under some well-defined path norm for path distance computation, $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} f_c(\sigma_n) = f_c(\sigma^*)$. An algorithm is asymptotically optimal if the minimum cost it finds converges to the optimal cost as the sample number approaches infinity. Since the graph construction procedure remains unchanged from the original versions in PRM$^*$, RRG, and their variants for PTOPP, the algorithms' asymptotic optimality is inherited because path costs are compatible. More specifically, the unchanged graph structures ensure that a sequence of paths converging to the optimal path exists with probability one for all large sampling numbers. Using the monotonicity of the best path cost with sample increasing, the minimum cost these algorithms return converges to $f_c(\sigma^*)$ almost surely. For PTOPP based on RRT$^*$, only the routines related to path cost computations are customized. It is guaranteed that the best path starting from $\mathbf{x}_0$ and reaching each new vertex $\mathbf{x}_i$ is found, and each vertex has a unique parent with the smallest cost. Then the asymptotical optimality follows. 


\subsubsection{Computational complexity} 
For graph construction, it has been shown that sampling-based optimal planners take $O(n \log n \log^d m)$ time under efficient implementations of core procedures such as collision check, nearest neighbor search, and range search. Concisely, the number of nearby nodes inside the search ball centered at the new node in iteration $n$ is of $O(\log n)$. They can be found in $O(\log n)$ time via approximate range search. Checking collisions between an edge and $m$ obstacles in $\mathbb{R}^d$ can be executed in $O(\log^d m)$ time with spatial trees. Summing up over $n$ iterations, the total computation time is of $O(n \log n \log^d m)$ \citep{karaman2011sampling}. However, note that the complexity of the subtree update routine in RRT$^*$ is not taken into account. The total edge number $|E|$ in RRT$^*$ is of $O(n)$, implying that the average edge change number in the rewiring procedure is of $O(1)$. As a graph traversal routine, the complexity of \texttt{SubtreeUpdate}() is determined by the node number of the subtree branch. RRT$^*$ maintains a tree similar to the random online nearest neighbor graph. 
% and arrives at a balanced tree at last. 
The expected subtree node number rooted at a random node is no more than $\log n$ \citep{lichev2024new}. The total complexity thus remains.
