\subsubsection{Passage detection and cell decomposition results}
\hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results}} showcases examples of passages and cells detected amid random obstacles in 2D maps. Passage regions are bounded by blue dashed lines. Red dashed lines are passage segments, i.e., distances, between obstacle pairs that have constructed valid passages. Two types of lines may overlap. The environment size is $1000 \times 600$. In \hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results a]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results a}}-\hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results c]{\ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results c}}, obstacles (triangles, rectangles) are of similar sizes controlled by an identical side length of 40 and are densified with an increasing obstacle number $m$. To get a complete map decomposition, environment walls are processed as obstacles in this part, and passages between adjacent walls are assumed at the four corners. In \hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results d]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results d}}-\hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results f]{\ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results f}}, obstacles additionally vary in size with side length changes in $[20, 60]$ to enrich randomness. Overall, the detected passages and cells through the proposed criterion are sparse and informative in indicating narrow regions. Passage regions depict confined locations accurately and provide more elaborate passage descriptions than simple segments. The shadow volume intersection definition in (\ref{Eqn: Passage Region}) endows passages with sizes insensitive to relative obstacle size differences even in the presence of significant size differences in \hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results d]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results d}}-\hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results f]{\ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results f}}.


Spatial passages detected in 3D scenes are shown in \hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D}}. Passage segments evolve into passage planes with valid height intervals between obstacles in 3D space, which resemble spatial gates. For clarity, passage region boundaries and passages incident to environment walls are not depicted. The dimension of the environment is $1000 \times 6000 \times 400$. \hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D a]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D a}}-\hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D c]{\ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D c}} plot passages only considering the obstacle distribution on the base. They start at zero height, i.e., the base, and have the upper height bound $\overline{h}$ equal to the lower obstacle forming them. This description is used in \citep{huang2024homotopic}. The corresponding passage distributions at all heights are shown in \hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D d]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D d}}-\hyperref[Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D f]{\ref{Fig: Passage and Cell Detection Results 3D f}} for comparison. Narrow passages are detected at all heights for a comprehensive description of places with restricted accessible free space. Passages traversed by paths in 3D space can thus be completely captured. Passages get sparser as height increases because fewer obstacles exist at large heights. 
% The sparsity of passages is well achieved since a passage's valid height interval usually goes across multiple height intervals formed by obstacles.
As is observed, complex decompositions of the environment are generated, making direct node positioning challenging. This necessitates the proposed node projection localization in base cells for rapid passage traversal check during planning.
