hus declines. 



Next, statistics of passage detection in 3D environments are reported in \hyperref[Fig: Passage And Cell Detection Data 3D]{Fig. \ref{Fig: Passage And Cell Detection Data 3D}}. As the impact of relative obstacle sizes has been investigated, the general case of varying obstacle sizes is adopted. Each obstacle is assigned a random height in $[0, 400]$, and the base ground remains the 2D dimension of $1000 \times 600$. For comparison, the passage and cell numbers on the base ground considering the planar obstacle distribution are listed. The data show that the 3D passage number is also linear in the obstacle number. In other words, $O(m)$ valid spatial passages exist. This linearity originates from the fact that all intermediate edges in the incremental Delaunay graph construction process are checked for passages by the Gabriel condition. The ratio of the spatial passage number to the base passage number is $1.7$ on average, suggesting good sparsity of spatial passages and that the 3D passage number is not significantly larger than that in the associated planner case. Beyond the planner setups, sparse passages are distributed in 3D space via the proposed detection condition, which is crucial to enabling efficient planning in PTOPP.


\subsubsection{Detection efficiency}
\hyperref[Fig: Passage Detection Time]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage Detection Time}}  and \hyperref[Tab: Passage and Cell Detection Time]{Table \ref{Tab: Passage and Cell Detection Time}} detail the passage and Gabriel cell detection time in experiments. Passage detection time using Delaunay graphs and the direct brute-force traversal strategy are reported. While data in both fixed and varying obstacle sizes are provided, the detection time is similar for the same obstacle number since obstacle size variations do not affect the detection complexity. How Delaunay graphs improve the detection efficiency and how the detection time varies with the obstacle number are of interest. Because of fast Delaunay graph construction and the subsequent linear passage check routine within a small geodesic distance in the graph, significant time is saved compared to directly traversing all obstacle pairs as demonstrated in \hyperref[Fig: Passage Detection Time Fixed Obstacle Sizes]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage Detection Time Fixed Obstacle Sizes}}, \hyperref[Fig: Passage Detection Varying Obstacle Sizes]{\ref{Fig: Passage Detection Varying Obstacle Sizes}}, and \hyperref[Tab: Passage and Cell Detection Time]{Table \ref{Tab: Passage and Cell Detection Time}}. This improvement becomes more obvious as the obstacle number increases. Up to an order of magnitude speedup is reached, e.g., when 200 obstacles are present. \hyperref[Fig: Passage Detection Time]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage Detection Time}} indicates that the passage detection time increasing trend in Delaunay graphs is much less drastic than the direct check method, which has a cubic time complexity in the worst case. Due to the dominance of linearity in $O(m\log m)$ complexity, linearity between the detection time and obstacle number is obvious when using Delaunay graphs.
