The subsequent Gabriel cell detection only performs sorting and DFS of the passage graph once. It is thus fast and costs less than \SI{0.1}{\ms} when the obstacle number is below 200. In preprocessing of the environment, most computations occur in passage detection and Gabriel cell decomposition accounts for negligible computations against passage detection. The passage detection time in 3D space is displayed in \hyperref[Fig: Passage Detection Varying 3D]{Figure \ref{Fig: Passage Detection Varying 3D}}  and \hyperref[Tab: Passage and Cell Detection Time]{Table \ref{Tab: Passage and Cell Detection Time}}. The corresponding detection time of planar passages on the base ground in each setup is listed as a benchmark. The results show that detecting spatial passages is more time-consuming than solely attaining the passages on the base because all intermediate edges in Delaunay graphs are examined. Although the computational complexity is unchanged, the detection time rising trend with the obstacle number is more drastic. Despite the time increase, it is efficient in contrast to separately checking passages in consecutive obstacle height intervals that conducts detection in $m$ height intervals and has $O(m^2\log m)$ complexity. Compound cell detection in 3D space takes a much longer running time than 2D cases. This is attributed to processing midair passages. For each midair passage, computation of the base cells its projection intersects with is executed. This accounts for most of the compound cell detection time. Overall, compound cell detection is completed fast within short durations and spends less time than 3D passage detection.


\subsection{PTOPP results}
After passage and Gabriel cell detection, all PTOPP categories and the dependent subroutines are implemented and thoroughly tested. This part reports PTOPP results and particularly focuses on accessible free space optimization performances characterized by different path cost formulations compared to benchmarks. As a new path planning paradigm leveraging passage properties, PTOPP requires new functionalities in planners that have not been realized before, e.g., passage detection and basic primitives in Section \ref{Sec: PTOPP Primitives}. Despite many efficient planner implementations available, e.g., the open motion planning library \citep{sucan2012open}, there are no existing interfaces for passage processing, traversal check, and cost configuration to develop PTOPP algorithms based on them readily. PTOPP planners are implemented independently with all subroutines in {\CC}. In particular, PRM$^*$ and RRT$^*$ for PTOPP are tested as representative sampling-based methods. PTOPP primitives are integrated as common components invokable by upper-layer planners. Built on these primitives, PTOPP categories are easily configurable in planner instances via specifying cost computation ways. The test environments cover 2D and 3D workspaces.  
Though the chief objective of PTOPP is to enable large accessible free space along paths, no explicit clearance to obstacles is embedded in the path planning stage. Paths are optimal in the sense of having large accessible free space, not clearance, available over their lengths. But clearance is easily achievable by postprocessing because large clearance is essentially admitted in PTOPP. For instance, raw paths may be locally translated to passage centers to enlarge clearance \citep{huang2023deformable, huang2024robot}. Such postprocessing can be done efficiently to make practically usable paths. For consistency, path clearance is not explicitly constrained in the planning stage of PTOPP.


\subsubsection{PTOPP results by categories}
\hyperref[Fig: Plannar Planning Examples]{Figure \ref{Fig: Plannar Planning Examples}} presents path planning examples in 2D maps in which RRT$^*$ for both MPW-PTOPP and GPW-PTOPP are conducted, and shortest path planning acts as the benchmark. The planned paths commonly differ in traversed passages in various planners. In planar environments, the differences essentially correspond to different path homotopy classes. Instead of being the shortest, paths in PTOPP pass selected passages to optimize accessible free space characterized by associated costs. The start and goal positions are placed at the top left and bottom right corners, respectively. 3-GPW-PTOPP is implemented with a weight item common ratio of $100$ in experiments. Obstacles of varying sizes respect uniform or Gaussian distributions for diverse distributions. Environment boundaries are processed as obstacles.  Each plan is run until the maximum valid sample number $| V | = 5$k, which is sufficient for optimal path convergence in tests. Raw paths may graze obstacles since no postprocessing for path clearance is carried out, but the accessibility of free space is not influenced.
