\subsubsection{PTOPP results in 3D space}
PTOPP algorithms are further tested in 3D environments, the most common workspace in robotics applications. In implementation, the major modifications come from spatial passage detection and compound Gabriel cell decomposition in preprocessing, and the adjusted node positioning procedure in planning. Node projections are positioned in the planar cells on the base for fast passage traversal check. To check if a path traverses midair passages, they are added as virtual boundaries of cells that their projections intersect with. To obtain the passed passages of an edge, both real and virtual cell boundaries are examined. In this way, passage traversal status in 3D space is efficiently computed in PTOPP.


\hyperref[Fig: 3D Planning Examples]{Figure \ref{Fig: 3D Planning Examples}} showcases PTOPP results in 3D space returned by RRT$^*$. Different planning categories are conducted in the same environment. The 3D environment dimension remains unchanged, and the boundaries are not depicted. The start and goal positions are set as the bottom right and top left corners respectively, with height going from 100 to 200. In addition to displacement on the x-y plane, the height goes up significantly. The maximum valid sample number increases to $|V| = 15$k. The shortest paths serve as a benchmark. PTOPP improves the free space accessibility in different ways. Specifically, obstacles of different heights restrict the free space, and the shortest paths usually traverse narrow passages to minimize the path length. MPW-PTOPP and GPW-PTOPP enlarge accessible free space by selecting wider passages to travel. Analogously to the planar case, MPW-PTOPP only optimizes the narrowest passage width. Although accessible free space is enlarged in MPW-PTOPP, GPW-PTOPP maximizes the top \textit{k} narrowest passages, making it more greedy in optimizing accessible free space. As demonstrated in \hyperref[Fig: 3D Planning Examples]{Figure \ref{Fig: 3D Planning Examples}}, paths returned by GPW-PTOPP ($\textit{k} = 3$) usually have longer lengths in order to pass wider passages. 


The paths additionally go up and down to traverse wide passages in 3D space. If $\mathbf{x}_0$ or $\mathbf{x}_g$ is at a relatively low height, the optimal paths prefer high heights because passages are sparse and wide there. This is exemplified in \hyperref[Fig: PTOPP Paths at High Heights]{Figure \ref{Fig: PTOPP Paths at High Heights}} where at least one of $\mathbf{x}_0$ and $\mathbf{x}_g$ has a low height. The optimal paths in PTOPP tend to go upwards first to reach a high height and then lead to the goal position. By doing this, the paths only go through a few wide passages at high heights, contributing to the minimized path cost. In practice, this strategy may be undesired if certain properties are expected from paths. For instance, robots need to traverse different heights sufficiently for information collection purposes in search tasks or have constrained height climb capabilities. To address this, the sampling strategy can be specified, such as limiting the admissible height range of samples. For this reason, the height range of sampling in \hyperref[Fig: 3D Planning Examples]{Figure \ref{Fig: 3D Planning Examples}} is set as $[0, 205]\T$ with the upper bound slightly larger than $\mathbf{x}_g$ height to prevent the optimal paths from going too high. At last, CPW-PTOPP is also tested in 3D space. As showcased in \hyperref[Fig: CPW-PTOPP Paths 3D]{Figure \ref{Fig: CPW-PTOPP Paths 3D}}, $\mathbf{x}_0$ and $\mathbf{x}_g$ remain the same as in \hyperref[Fig: 3D Planning Examples]{Figure \ref{Fig: 3D Planning Examples}}. The impact of a constrained passage width is similar to planar cases. If imposed in shortest path planning, it avoids spatial passages narrower than the admissible width ($\underline{\varepsilon}_p = 60)$ as much as possible, i.e., passing no such passages or the fewest possible. If imposed in PTOPP, it minimizes the number of passed passages whose widths are smaller than $\underline{\varepsilon}_p$.
