In \hyperref[Fig: Path Planning Time Obstacle]{Figure \ref{Fig: Path Planning Time Obstacle}}, the proposed PTOPP algorithms manifest close computational efficiency with the shortest path planning and maximum clearance planning, whereas direct passage traversal check in PTOPP is much more time-consuming. In RRT$^*$-based PTOPP using passage traversal, nearly twice the planning time of the shortest path planning baseline is cost in both 2D and 3D space (depicted by purple solid lines in \hyperref[Fig: Path Planning Time Obstacle]{Figure \ref{Fig: Path Planning Time Obstacle}}). In PRM$^*$-based PTOPP, increases in running time are also dramatic. This presents a bottleneck in PTOPP computational efficiency, hindering its applicability in situations of limited computational resources, e.g., onboard systems. The proposed algorithms address this issue effectively. Overall, there is no significant planning time rises in PTOPP as shown by green lines in \hyperref[Fig: Path Planning Time Obstacle]{Figure \ref{Fig: Path Planning Time Obstacle}}. The average planning time increments of PTOPP over the shortest path planning baseline are \SI{1.7}{\percent} in PRM$^*$ and \SI{7.4}{\percent} in RRT$^*$ for 2D space, and \SI{5.3}{\percent} and \SI{24.2}{\percent} respectively for 3D space, substantially lower than direct passage traversal. Despite the additional computation loads, PTOPP achieves comparable computational efficiency to the baseline. Compared to other accessible free space optimization methods, its performance is similar to maximum clearance planning and mostly superior to clearance constraint planning, especially amid dense obstacles. 


\subsubsection{Planning efficiency with different sample numbers and cost evolution}
Lastly, the planning time with different valid sample numbers is shown in \hyperref[Fig: Path Planning Time Sample]{Figure \ref{Fig: Path Planning Time Sample}}. The environment is populated with 40 obstacles. The planning time is averaged over five runs. Direct passage traversal PTOPP takes significantly more time than other planners as the sample number increases, while the proposed PTOPP dramatically reduces the time expense. In PRM$^*$-based methods, its performance is better or close to the benchmarks of clearance constraint and maximum clearance planning. In RRT$^*$, it consumes slightly more time than benchmarks only when the valid sample number gets extremely large. For planning time trends, RRT$^*$-based planners exhibit stronger linearity in the obstacle number, whereas the linearity of running time in the valid sample number is more evident in PRM$^*$-based planners. PRM$^*$-based planners also take shorter planning time when the sample number is large. \hyperref[Fig: Cost Evolution]{Figure \ref{Fig: Cost Evolution}} illustrates the evolution of costs of the best paths during planning to highlight the fast convergence of PTOPP. Because MPW-PTOPP and maximum clearance planning find homotopic optimal paths in 2D space, they are compared together with the shortest path planning baseline. MPW-PTOPP converges to the optimal cost much faster than others due to the discreteness of its cost. Finite cost values exist in PTOPP and thus can be explored rapidly, as illustrated by stages in \hyperref[Fig: Cost Evolution 2D RRTS]{Figure \ref{Fig: Cost Evolution 2D RRTS}} and \hyperref[Fig: Cost Evolution 3D RRTS]{\ref{Fig: Cost Evolution 3D RRTS}}. Clearance also gets updated periodically in RRT$^*$, but is less efficiently explored for its value continuity. PRM$^*$-based planners demonstrate faster convergence than RRT$^*$ counterparts because random roadmaps can explore a much larger configuration space partition than random trees in RRT$^*$ given the same number of samples. Random trees only grow from leaves in the sampling process. Note that the maximum clearance in PRM$^*$ can decrease with increasing samples. This is attributed to that the near neighbor search radius $r_{PRM^*}$ is set to decrease with samples. As a consequence, samples of large clearance may not be reachable from path nodes given a small $r_{PRM^*}$. To address this, a large $r_{PRM^*}$ is required for dense samples at the cost of higher time consumption.


\section{Discussion and conclusion}
Passages provide advantages over clearance in accessible free space measurement. Many approaches to enlarging path clearance, such as path search in Voronoi graphs and path postprocessing, are unable to globally maximize clearance. Though maximum clearance planning can be employed, the degradation problem exists as in MPW-PTOPP. Since path clearance values are continuous, they cannot be optimized the same as discrete passage widths in PTOPP. Passage graphs offer a new perspective to organize objects potential in scene understanding. Narrow passage regions may be used to guide sampling within for efficient path finding. The Gabriel condition is proposed for passage detection because of its intuitive physical interpretation in robotics contexts. Another possible choice of proximity graph is the relative neighborhood graph (RNG), a subgraph of the Gabriel graph \citep{toussaint1980relative, melchert2013percolation}. RNG only maintains the shortest edges of Delaunay triangles, imposing a stricter passage criterion than the Gabriel condition. The present pipeline of passage detection and cell decomposition still applies. Beyond the 2D and 3D workspace, the concept of passages is promising in higher dimensional spaces, e.g., the joint space, in describing confined configurations. But efficient characterization of subjects, e.g., obstacles, in high dimensional space is complicated \citep{dai2024certified}.


Accessible free space optimization for paths is studied in this article as one most direct and important application of PTOPP. Other PTOPP problem instances exist since PTOPP has good configurability and applicability to formulate and solve various problems related to passages. For example, passages may be abstracted as toll stations that incur costs to pass. For good path smoothness, one may minimize the number of traversed passages. The principles of compatible path costs provide important guidance for cost design in OPP. The presented algorithmic pipeline of PTOPP is general and aligns with sampling-based optimal planners. Thus, common techniques to accelerate planning and improve the solution quality, such as bidirectional planning and biasing samples to the goal region, are applicable. Apart from sampling-based methods, pure cell-based path planning methods are enabled by Gabriel cells. They can reach a much faster planning speed with no need for a large number of random samples. Their dependent procedures, however, are covered in the proposed algorithms.


This article presents PTOPP as a new path planning paradigm able to optimize traversed passages of paths. Accessible free space optimization is investigated as an important instantiation for which the entire PTOPP pipeline is developed systematically. In preprocessing of environments, passage detection and free space decomposition based on Gabriel graphs of obstacles are introduced. This enables fast detection of sparse and informative passage distributions and cell decompositions in 2D and 3D space. Typical PTOPP categories defined by path costs are then formulated, in which the compatibility requirements for path costs in sampling-based optimal algorithms are addressed. Next, the primitive procedures PTOPP relies on are elaborated. The core module for planning efficiency is rapid passage traversal check achieved by positioning samples in partitioned cells. Finally, sampling-based optimal algorithms for PTOPP are reported with detailed analysis of probabilistic completeness, asymptotic optimality, and computational complexity. Extensive experimental results verify the effectiveness and efficiency of PTOPP. It not only admits more versatile solutions to various planning problems but also demonstrates superior or comparable efficiency over existing methods. PTOPP offers new insights and tools to resolve fundamental path planning requirements not limited to free space accessibility.
Important future directions include extending current methods to more challenging planning setups, e.g, dynamic environments and higher dimensional configuration spaces, developing theories and planning methods for non-compatible path cost optimization, and deploying PTOPP in downstream robotic tasks, e.g., manipulation planning and navigation in confined environments.

