\subsection{Geodesic distance traversed in Delaunay graphs in passage detection}
\label{Appendix: Geodesic Distance Test}
When extracting the Gabriel graph from a Delaunay graph, it suffices to check all the Delaunay graph edges if the graph nodes are simple points. For edge $(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{x}_j)$, the graph nodes that possibly violate the Gabriel condition are also restricted to nodes adjacent to $\mathbf{x}_i$ or $\mathbf{x}_j$. When determining passage candidates from the Delaunay graph $\mathcal{DG}(C_c)$ constructed from the obstacle centroids set $C_c$, solely examining edges of $\mathcal{DG}(C_c)$ may miss some passages because obstacles are volumetric. The solution is to increase the check range defined by the geodesic distance, which is one in the point node case. The smallest geodesic distance $k_{gd}$ in $\mathcal{DG}(C_c)$ is described in (\ref{Eqn: Smallest Geodesic Distance}) and adopted as two in our implementation, which proves to be sufficient to find all passages under test conditions. The major factors influencing $k_{gd}$ include dimension and distribution properties of obstacles. The more obstacles can be treated as simple points (e.g., with small sizes and in large environments), the smaller $k_{gd}$ is. 


Here we report data on detected passage numbers in various setups with $k_{gd} = 1$ and $2$. It empirically verifies that a small $k_{gd} > 1$ is sufficient to detect all passages in most cases. As demonstrated in \hyperref[Tab: Passage Geodesic Distance Data]{Table \ref{Tab: Passage Geodesic Distance Data}} and \ref{Tab: Passage Geodesic Distance Percentage Data}, when obstacle sizes have limited variations, $k_{gd} = 2$ reports all passages in all trials. This corresponds to the side length range of $[20, 60]$ used in experiments. As expected, only checking edges of $\mathcal{DG}(C_c)$, i.e., $k_{gd} = 1$, fails to find all passages in most tests. If obstacle sizes have extreme variations, $k_{gd} = 2$ may also fail. When the obstacle side length range is set as $[1, 60]$, obstacle sizes may differ by thousands of times. Failure to detect all passages occurs occasionally for $k_{gd} = 2$, especially when obstacles are dense. Importantly, only a negligible number of passages are missed in failure cases as listed in \hyperref[Tab: Passage Geodesic Distance Percentage Data]{Table \ref{Tab: Passage Geodesic Distance Percentage Data}}, which is acceptable in most practical scenes. To find all passages, $k_{gd}$ only needs to be larger than two. $k_{gd}$ does not change the detection complexity because the expected degree of a Delaunay graph node is $O(1)$ and at most six \citep{mark2008computational}. The computational efficiency is guaranteed to outperform the brute-force check strategy.
