The training process employs several strategies to ensure robust learning. We use mean absolute error (MAE) as the loss function:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \text{MAE}(\hat{y}, y)
\end{equation}
where $\hat{y}$ represents predicted soil moisture and $y$ represents SMAP observations. To ensure independent evaluation, we split data at the ensemble level rather than randomly sampling individual timesteps, preventing data leakage between training and test sets. The AdamW optimizer with learning rate decay provides stable convergence, while early stopping with 20 epochs patience prevents overfitting during the training process.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Drought Case Study}

The NoahMP-AI framework demonstrates significant improvements in soil moisture prediction accuracy during the 2022 Texas drought compared to standalone Noah-MP simulations. The framework achieves substantial error reductions, with mean absolute error decreasing from 0.0642 to 0.0313 m³/m³ and root mean square error improving from 0.0831 to 0.0440 m³/m³ (approx. 50\% reduction). Analysis across all test ensemble members reveals that NoahMP-AI outperformed Noah-MP at every single timestep (112/112), demonstrating consistent enhancement in predictive skill while preserving physically meaningful spatial and temporal patterns.

The spatial bias analysis (Figure \ref{fig:spatial_comparison}) reveals the geographic distribution of model improvements. Noah-MP exhibits pronounced wet biases (blue regions) across southern and central Texas, with scattered dry biases (red regions) throughout the domain. NoahMP-AI effectively reduces these systematic errors, producing bias patterns much closer to zero (white regions) across the majority of the domain while maintaining spatial coherence.

