\begin{abstract}
% Smart glasses are emerging as the next-generation interface for ubiquitous access to large language models (LLMs). However, enabling reliable interaction with LLMs in noisy environments remains a major challenge, particularly due to interference from side speech. In this paper, we propose a novel framework to enhance LLM robustness using the recently released LLaMA 4 model, with the following key innovations: (1) Multi-microphone input is simultaneously fed into LLaMA 4, granting the model spatial awareness and enabling it to suppress side speech effectively; (2) Instead of fine-tuning LLaMA 4 using LoRA, we introduce a cascaded model architecture that prepends a lightweight encoder module. This design promotes modular deployment and facilitates iterative model updates; (3) We incorporate a multi-scale Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) strategy, addressing both frame-level and utterance-level side speech suppression. Experimental results show that our multi-channel LLaMA 4 system can eliminate up to 96\% of side speech and reduce word error rate (WER) by 50\% in noisy scenarios.

Smart glasses are increasingly positioned as the next-generation interface for ubiquitous access to large language models (LLMs). Nevertheless, achieving reliable interaction in real-world noisy environments remains a major challenge, particularly due to interference from side speech. In this work, we introduce a novel side-talk rejection multi-microphone Whisper (MMW) framework for smart glasses, incorporating three key innovations. First, we propose a Mix Block based on a Tri-Mamba architecture to effectively fuse multi-channel audio at the raw waveform level, while maintaining compatibility with streaming processing. Second, we design a Frame Diarization Mamba Layer to enhance frame-level side-talk suppression, facilitating more efficient fine-tuning of Whisper models. Third, we employ a Multi-Scale Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) strategy to jointly optimize frame-level and utterance-level side speech suppression. Experimental evaluations demonstrate that the proposed MMW system can reduce the word error rate (WER) by 4.95\% in noisy conditions.

\end{abstract}
